<h2>1. Saṅkhyā: Numbers in Sanskrit</h2>
<p>
  Sanskrit numbers (<strong>saṅkhyā, संख्या</strong>) are the ancestors of number words in many languages: <code>त्रि</code> (three), <code>सप्त</code> (seven) and <code>दश</code> (ten) are related to English <em>three</em>, Latin <em>septem</em> and <em>decem</em>. The digits we write today also descend from the numerals of ancient India. In Devanāgarī they are written ० १ २ ३ ४ ५ ६ ७ ८ ९.
</p>

<h2>2. Counting from 1 to 10</h2>
<p>
  When simply counting, the neuter forms are used:
</p>
<Data kind="cardinals" headers={["Digit", "Sanskrit"]} />

<h2>3. From 11 to 20</h2>
<p>
  The teens are formed like English "-teen": unit + <code>दश</code>. Note the sound changes in <code>द्वादश</code> (12), <code>त्रयोदश</code> (13) and <code>षोडश</code> (16). Nineteen can also be expressed as "one less than twenty": <code>एकोनविंशतिः</code>.
</p>
<Data kind="teens" headers={["Digit", "Sanskrit"]} />

<h2>4. Tens, Hundreds and Thousands</h2>
<Data kind="tens" headers={["Digit", "Sanskrit"]} />
<p>
  Numbers in between are built by placing the unit before the ten: <code>एकविंशतिः</code> (21), <code>पञ्चविंशतिः</code> (25), <code>अष्टचत्वारिंशत्</code> (48).
</p>

<h2>5. Numbers and Gender</h2>
<p>
  Numbers behave like adjectives, but in different ways:
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><strong>1 to 4</strong> change for gender (and case) to agree with the noun.</li>
  <li><strong>5 to 18</strong> have the same form in all genders: <code>पञ्च बालकाः</code>, <code>पञ्च बालिकाः</code>, <code>पञ्च फलानि</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>19 and above</strong> are nouns in their own right; <code>विंशतिः</code> and its relatives are feminine singular: <code>विंशतिः छात्राः</code> (twenty students).</li>
</ul>
<Data kind="gender" headers={["Number", "Masculine", "Feminine", "Neuter"]} />
<p>
  Examples: <code>एकः बालकः</code>, <code>एका बालिका</code>, <code>एकं फलम्</code>; <code>त्रयः छात्राः</code>, <code>तिस्रः छात्राः</code> (feminine), <code>त्रीणि पुस्तकानि</code>.
</p>

<h2>6. Ordinal Numbers</h2>
<Data kind="ordinals" headers={["Number", "Masculine", "Feminine"]} />
<p>
  Ordinals agree with their noun in gender, number and case, like any adjective: <code>प्रथमः पाठः</code> (the first lesson), <code>द्वितीया कक्षा</code> (the second class).
</p>

<h2>7. Telling the Time</h2>
<p>
  Clock time uses the word <strong>वादनम्</strong> ("o'clock", literally "striking"), compounded with the number. To ask the time, say <code>कः समयः?</code> or <code>अधुना कति वादनम्?</code>
</p>
<ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
  <li><code>सपाद</code> (+¼): quarter past</li>
  <li><code>सार्ध</code> (+½): half past</li>
  <li><code>पादोन</code> (−¼): quarter to the <em>next</em> hour</li>
  <li><code>अधिक</code> / <code>ऊन</code>: minutes past / minutes to</li>
</ul>
<Data kind="time" headers={["Time", "Sanskrit"]} />
<p>
  To say "at" a time, put <code>वादन</code> in the 7th case: <code>अहं षड्वादने उत्तिष्ठामि</code> (I get up at six o'clock); <code>विद्यालयः सार्धसप्तवादने आरभते</code> (school begins at half past seven).
</p>
<p>
  Time-telling is a regular question in CBSE Sanskrit papers, so practise reading a few clock faces aloud every day.
</p>
